.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_create_mp \- allocate and initialize an HTTP request with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttp_request_t *chttp_request_create_mp(chttp_method_t method,
                                         const char *url,
                                         const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                                         ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                                         char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function allocates a new chttp_request_t. It copies the URL into
a buffer that the request owns, and does the same with the body data
and the content_type string when they are not NULL, so the caller can
free its own originals as soon as this call returns.

The request's method, expect_continue and
prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect fields are public to read and to
write. The url and body fields are public to read only, because the
request owns the copies they point to: this function allocates them
with mprocs and chttp_request_free frees them with mprocs. Do not
assign to url or to any member of body after construction; build
another request when you need another URL or another body.

Pass NULL for body, or use the CHTTP_NO_BODY macro, when the request
has no body, as with GET and DELETE. body->data can be NULL only when
body->len == 0. A NULL body->data with a body->len that is not zero is
rejected and the function returns NULL, instead of taking the pair as a
request with no body.

Only CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT and CHTTP_PATCH send a body. With any other
method the client never sends the body, and sends no Content-Length
and no Content-Type either. A POST, PUT or PATCH without a body goes
out with "Content-Length: 0".

The expect_continue field of a new request is false, since the library
zero-fills the struct. If you set it to true after construction (for
example req->expect_continue = true), the client sends "Expect:
100-continue" and waits for the server's interim response before it
sends the body; chttpclient_do(3) describes the full protocol. The
field has no effect on a request without a body, or on a request that
already has an explicit "Expect" header from chttp_request_set_header.
Every tier honors it the same way: chttpclient_do and
chttpclient_do_streaming, chttpclient_do_async and
chttpclient_do_async_streaming, and chttpclient_do_pooled and
chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method    The HTTP method (CHTTP_GET, CHTTP_POST and so on). The
          function refuses CHTTP_ANY, which is a server route
          wildcard, and every value outside the enum.
url       The target URL. It must not be NULL. The function copies it
          into the request.
body      A pointer to a chttp_request_body_t, or NULL for no body.
mprocs    A custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free.
err_str   Optional. It gets a static error string if the call fails.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new request, or NULL if the call fails. The call
fails for a NULL url, for a method that names no HTTP method
(CHTTP_ANY included), for a NULL body->data with a body->len that is
not zero, and when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Every allocation goes through mprocs when mprocs is not NULL.
The request must stay alive until every chttpclient_do call that uses
it has returned, because a synchronous call like that reads the
request's method, body and headers directly.

body->content_type is copied here as it is, without a check, because
at this point the function cannot know whether that value will ever
reach the wire: an explicit "content-type" header that you set later
with chttp_request_set_header takes its place. If body->content_type
holds a CR or an LF byte when the request is serialized, or any other
byte below 0x20 except a horizontal tab, or 0x7F, the chttpclient_do,
chttp_run_query or other call that uses this request fails with
ccol_invalid_args, and the value is never written to the wire.
chttp_request_set_header(3) explains why.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_create (3),
.BR chttp_request_set_header (3),
.BR chttp_request_free (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3)
